Given, $Q_{1}=2 \mu \mathrm{C}=2 \times 10^{-6} \mathrm{C}$
Q_{2}=1 \mu $\mathrm{C}=1 \times10^{-6} \mathrm{C}$ The electric field intensity at a point $P$ due to a point charge is given by $\begin{aligned} E &=\frac{1}{4 \pi \varepsilon_{0}} \frac{Q}{r^{2}}\\
\Rightarrow \quad & E \propto \frac{Q}{r^{2}}
\end{aligned}\therefore$ Ratio of electric fields due to $Q_{1} and Q_{2}$ at point $P$ is $\begin{aligned}\frac{E_{1}}{E_{2}}&=\frac{Q_{1}}{r_{1}^{2}} \times \frac{r_{2}^{2}}{Q_{2}}\\
